I'm investing more today in tokens than in knowledge workers.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:06,880
We could build superhuman agents.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,540
This means that by every dimension that matters,

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:13,280
our agents would outperform the best human we had ever hired.

5
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:16,220
The most ambitious companies listening to this will decide to follow suit,

6
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:20,600
which is you decided to build an agent per customer.

7
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:26,200
Every day, between 100 and 200,000 agents get instantiated

8
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:29,060
specifically for this customer with its own virtual machine.

9
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:33,880
There's a lot of people worried about how the organizations of the future are going to look like

10
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:36,080
and the role that humans are going to play.

11
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:40,760
If you haven't faced fear before, you haven't felt it, then you haven't tried AI.

12
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:44,700
We launched a program inside Kavak that's called the Jedi Academy.

13
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:49,260
From the CEO to like AI engineers to mechanics, we train everyone.

14
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:53,620
And after six weeks, they launch state-of-the-art agents to production.

15
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:57,820
What advice do you have to future founders or first-time founders that might be listening?

16
00:00:57,820 --> 00:00:59,460
What works right now is...

17
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:04,530
Welcome back to the a16z podcast.

18
00:01:05,010 --> 00:01:07,490
Today, we have Ale Maza, the head of AI at Kavak.

19
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:11,370
We're going to discuss today the transformation that Ale led within Kavak

20
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:13,110
to turn into an AI-native company.

21
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:15,350
Thank you, Ale, for being with us today.

22
00:01:15,470 --> 00:01:16,210
Thanks for having me.

23
00:01:16,430 --> 00:01:20,970
Before starting at Kavak, you were running a company called Opi Analytics.

24
00:01:21,370 --> 00:01:21,730
That's right.

25
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:26,470
And you were very much into AI before ChatGPT.

26
00:01:26,630 --> 00:01:28,150
You want to tell us a little bit about that journey?

27
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:29,130
Yes, yes, of course.

28
00:01:29,270 --> 00:01:31,670
Well, we called it machine learning back then.

29
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:33,650
It was a different family of algorithms.

30
00:01:33,910 --> 00:01:41,440
And we founded a company with this very ambitious vision there that new machine learning models

31
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:45,900
would be so powerful that they could solve any complex problem.

32
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:47,840
This was pre-Transformers, right?

33
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:49,700
This was like 2013.

34
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:51,980
So we started building the company that way.

35
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:57,600
And I think we were like 10 years ahead of time, but we built a great company.

36
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:05,640
We served like 1,400, 500 companies around like risk algorithms, logistics, forecasting, marketing.

37
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:18,340
But really the power of what Transformers and then the ChatGPT moment when it arrived make things very clearly

38
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:29,260
that we could now build a whole new company and way of building companies.

39
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:33,440
And we joined Kavak and Carlos to build it.

40
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:34,320
Amazing.

41
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:34,580
All right.

42
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:39,580
So we're going to spend the bulk of this podcast talking about exactly how you've identified Kavak.

43
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:43,200
But maybe just to start, what does Kavak do and what is your role there?

44
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:47,200
Kavak started out as a used car marketplace.

45
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:53,360
So we buy cars, we refurbish them, and then we sell them and finance them.

46
00:02:53,440 --> 00:03:02,440
But to do that, we also had to build a fintech and a logistics company and the Carfax and like

47
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:06,440
basically all the infrastructure for this to work didn't exist in LATAM.

48
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:10,940
So we had to build everything vertically so we could serve our customers the right way.

49
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:14,260
I'm going to sort of start with the framing of what the architecture looks like.

50
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:17,080
So a consumer comes in and says, I want to sell my car.

51
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,340
Like, how many agents do they touch?

52
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:20,900
Like, what's the harness look like?

53
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:22,340
Like, ground us in how you design this.

54
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:22,560
Right.

55
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:27,940
So we bet the company in transforming to a company run by agents.

56
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:37,360
The questions we ask ourselves is, how would we build Kavak in 2035 with Fable 10 or GPT-10 level intelligence?

57
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:43,740
And actually, that company looks very different than what we had built or what we had back then.

58
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:52,350
So when a customer comes in right now, agent will get spawned specifically for this customer with its own virtual machine.

59
00:03:52,350 --> 00:04:01,370
It will remember years of interaction of these customers with Kavak, what they visited in the webpage or a call they had two years ago.

60
00:04:01,370 --> 00:04:14,190
So remember everything like in its memory, come up with a strategy and set a long-term goal to maximize the lifetime value of this customer and do whatever it takes to make the customer happy.

61
00:04:14,190 --> 00:04:20,830
And convert them into, like, all our different products, like, across time.

62
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:31,290
And this is a completely new and groundbreaking architecture at scale, I think, because, like, people are still building multi-agent system with experts.

63
00:04:31,290 --> 00:04:44,090
And we realized to bet that long-running agents with hard goals, not just workflows, could maximize our customers' satisfaction and, obviously, their lifetime value.

64
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:45,160
Awesome.

65
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:45,660
Okay.

66
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:47,780
So we're going to jump to the nuances of that.

67
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:53,880
But maybe versus many companies that say, hey, we want to be agentic, and they try some workflows.

68
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:58,320
You guys took the just rip, like, we had to make this work.

69
00:04:58,420 --> 00:04:59,760
You had to downsize dramatically.

70
00:05:00,060 --> 00:05:02,540
It didn't work for a year.

71
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:02,920
Right.

72
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:07,460
So do you want to talk through, obviously, you had to tune a lot of things to make that work.

73
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:12,480
Like, describe the harness at that time and, like, what models you were using and sort of specifically.

74
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:16,920
So there were, like, three main decisions that we had to make.

75
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:23,520
The first, and this is where I think many companies are stuck right now, is the first instinct is, okay,

76
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:24,840
let's adopt AI.

77
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:31,000
And you basically leave your structure as it is and just give ChatGPT or Claude to your team.

78
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:33,340
And then there's no efficiencies.

79
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:36,520
Your customers have the same problems and nothing happens, right?

80
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:43,400
And so you need to redesign your whole company around the agents and around the future capabilities.

81
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:52,580
And this means really, like, rebuilding most of your APIs, rebuilding your system so the agents can use them to perform.

82
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:57,860
Then you need to start generating the data and the feedback loops to fine-tune these agents.

83
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:01,560
The only way to really make them work is if you teach them.

84
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:02,360
Like, how do you teach them?

85
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:04,400
You put them out in the open.

86
00:06:04,580 --> 00:06:05,900
You put them in front of customers.

87
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:06,720
You get that data.

88
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:07,560
You get those evals.

89
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:11,800
And then you train your agents.

90
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:17,560
And this is the second bet that we made, that we could build superhuman agents.

91
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:24,820
This means that by every dimension that matters, like conversion, lifetime value, customer experience,

92
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:29,120
our agents would outperform the best human we had ever hired.

93
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:30,940
And we put them in front of the hardest problems.

94
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:37,280
And finally, you start to change how you measure the success of the company.

95
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:39,940
Kavak was a transactional company.

96
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:46,940
We used to measure how many cars we bought, how many cars we sold, how many brakes we needed to, brake pads we needed to buy.

97
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:59,260
And we moved to a relational company where now I have 10 million customers in my database and I have agents assigned to most of them with the task of maximizing their lifetime value.

98
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:05,270
Now, we're selling cars and personal loans and very high-ticket items.

99
00:07:05,550 --> 00:07:15,010
So just activating 1% of this customer base, it's like hundreds of millions of dollars if we do it the right way.

100
00:07:15,010 --> 00:07:21,670
So it's a bet that made sense for us because of our industry, because of the ticket, and because at the end of the day,

101
00:07:22,010 --> 00:07:27,420
customers need to build trust with a company because they're buying a used car.

102
00:07:27,900 --> 00:07:34,800
And the way to build trust is to know them and to plan and nurture a long-term relationship.

103
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:37,180
Ale, I just wanted to double-click on something.

104
00:07:37,380 --> 00:07:39,680
You know, evals over agent demos.

105
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:40,260
Yeah.

106
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:47,320
You probably get pitched a lot of agents and, you know, it's never been easier to build things like before.

107
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:51,380
But one of the questions is, like, how do you guys go about evaluating this?

108
00:07:51,420 --> 00:07:56,640
Because not everybody tests them across 90% of the customer interactions to see if they're really working.

109
00:07:56,960 --> 00:08:04,100
And, you know, you guys, I believe, is about 98% of the interactions or something like that are now handled by agents.

110
00:08:04,100 --> 00:08:04,840
Yes, totally.

111
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:13,960
So to give you a sense of the scale, like 96% of all interactions are handled by agents.

112
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:16,140
So no humans there.

113
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:21,640
Like 95% of all transactions are completely handled by agents.

114
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:25,900
Obviously, you meet a human when you pick up your car, like there's someone physically there to give you the keys.

115
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:31,120
But the rest of the experience of the journey is handled by an agent.

116
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:38,320
Every day, between 100,000 and 200,000 agents get instantiated in a day.

117
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:39,540
They wake up.

118
00:08:39,860 --> 00:08:44,680
They work sometimes for three minutes, sometimes for eight hours, sometimes for three days.

119
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:49,400
And they, like, set an alarm clock for their next task and go back to sleep.

120
00:08:49,660 --> 00:08:52,960
So the scale of this is just amazing.

121
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:53,960
And it's working.

122
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:57,300
Like, now, how do you get this to work at scale?

123
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:01,200
And the answer you mentioned is evals.

124
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:04,740
Like, I like to move extremely fast.

125
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:08,160
But in order to move fast, you need to have brakes, right?

126
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:08,840
Imagine a car.

127
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:13,560
You'll hit on the gas just if you have the right brakes.

128
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:15,700
And AI is super powerful.

129
00:09:15,860 --> 00:09:22,340
And I've seen many companies get this wrong because they try to go slow because they don't have the right brakes.

130
00:09:22,340 --> 00:09:24,360
So I thought about it the other way around.

131
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:25,880
Like, how fast can we go?

132
00:09:26,420 --> 00:09:28,400
Well, it depends on the quality of our evals.

133
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:43,100
So a good rule of thumb here is we spend about the same amount of time, engineer time, tokens, and money on building the evals than building the agents.

134
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:48,260
And this is how you get better and better and better, not letting evals as an afterthought.

135
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:49,560
So what do we measure?

136
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:53,420
First and foremost, like, the results for the business.

137
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:56,460
Like, if my customer is happy, they'll buy a car.

138
00:09:56,460 --> 00:09:59,060
They'll get their loan approved.

139
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:01,080
They'll sell a car to us.

140
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:03,480
And that's the, like, first check.

141
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:05,020
Like, did it convert?

142
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:07,800
And that's where most things break.

143
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:20,440
Like, I see companies, like, measuring number of calls or minutes during the call or some, like, superficial KPIs that give you some information, but that doesn't really work.

144
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:30,390
Like, the important thing is did this customer convert, is it bringing value to the customer, and is the customer happy to re-engage with us after a while?

145
00:10:30,730 --> 00:10:41,910
And once you get those evals connected, then it's just optimizing the right agentic architecture and giving the agent skills to scale this and cater to millions of customers.

146
00:10:43,010 --> 00:10:43,930
It's really, really amazing.

147
00:10:43,930 --> 00:11:02,280
And, you know, related to this is, like, okay, so you create the right evals, you know, it's working, you know, some people, some companies still feel a little bit risk-averse and putting them in front of the customers and being able to perform the highest leverage tasks, which in your case would be selling.

148
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:04,800
Do your agents really sell to customers?

149
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:05,200
Yes.

150
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:08,920
So we never built customer support or customer service agents.

151
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:11,540
We built, like, sales agents.

152
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:15,860
It's extremely hard to sell a car in Latin America.

153
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:18,760
So imagine someone wanting to buy a car.

154
00:11:19,140 --> 00:11:22,460
They can choose, like, amongst, like, 20,000 SKUs.

155
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:29,500
Then they need to pick, like, financing and go through the financing process, insurance and coverage.

156
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:32,680
And then they're probably trading in their car.

157
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:34,580
So we need to quote that car.

158
00:11:34,580 --> 00:11:48,160
So it's a process that if someone does it or the way Kavak did it back in 2020, 2021, was you need to be extremely good at 15 different things and have 15 different experts in 15 different teams.

159
00:11:48,160 --> 00:12:00,840
And usually the person would go and speak with the expert in financing, the expert in car advisory, the expert in buying, the expert in insurance, and they'll build a package and buy a car.

160
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:03,210
And that's extremely hard to do.

161
00:12:03,850 --> 00:12:19,360
But, like, the first thing we did was, okay, can we get an agent to be better than the expert in each of these things and then put it together and have, like, a mega expert that's an expert in insurance, financing, et cetera.

162
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:22,340
And that's who we put in front of the customer.

163
00:12:22,460 --> 00:12:24,140
So the experience for the customer is amazing.

164
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:31,580
We tripled NPS and customer satisfaction score by putting the agent in front of the customer.

165
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:37,100
And at first it converted, like, 50% more than our human team.

166
00:12:37,300 --> 00:12:43,360
And now it's converting over that, like, 2.1x more.

167
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:45,600
So it's a completely different company.

168
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:46,920
So your agents are better sellers.

169
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:47,860
Totally better.

170
00:12:47,860 --> 00:12:59,420
And you get this, right, because they're experts, and they're infinitely patient, and they know all your history, and they can plan for the long term, and they never get tired.

171
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:09,060
So, and if they make a mistake, they learn it, and the next day, not just them, but the other 200,000 agents will have learned from that mistake.

172
00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:16,340
So that's the feedback loop that we engaged, and that's showing in the growth and results and satisfaction of our customers.

173
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:25,160
One of the, well, two of the very cool things I think about Kavak is I think the world has gotten comfortable with AI can do customer service.

174
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:26,580
It's still very hard to do well.

175
00:13:26,580 --> 00:13:34,920
But, you know, as Gabe said, there's still a view that, well, customers aren't going to want to buy expensive things from AI, and you are proving them wrong.

176
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:35,180
Yes.

177
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:42,480
The next layer on that is, well, you're not actually going to be able to do regulated financial services end-to-end with AI.

178
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:51,280
But if you walk through what you're doing, you are underwriting a thin or no-file customer, pricing them correctly, doing servicing.

179
00:13:51,480 --> 00:14:04,680
So maybe talk through how did you write the evals to get comfortable with that, and then versus, I don't know, going to a bank branch or even a fintech, sort of how is that experience that much better?

180
00:14:04,680 --> 00:14:18,320
So the first financial product that we launched was a car loan, and usually in Mexico and in some emerging markets, it'll get like two months or more to get a car loan approved.

181
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:29,600
We usually approve it in under three minutes, which is like pretty cool, because we have all this data around the customer and the car.

182
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:45,220
And if the customer can't pay for the car anymore, they'll just return it to us, and we can give them a cheaper car, and then they pay a smaller amount each month, and they like get out of the water, which is amazing about the vertical integration of the business.

183
00:14:45,220 --> 00:14:54,660
But then, like, when we started launching other financial products, we realized that this is a very important decision for the customer, right?

184
00:14:54,740 --> 00:15:08,900
Like, they usually take three to four months to make up their mind in buying a car and getting a loan or getting a personal loan, like a large personal loan that we also do.

185
00:15:08,900 --> 00:15:22,100
So if you get to know your customer throughout this process and make the process easy for them, then just your conversion and retention metrics start going through the roof.

186
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:49,760
It's not just the transaction, it's understanding each customer personally and get them to convert when they're ready with a very deep personalization of the interest rate, the risk, the max amount of the loan in a way that makes sense for the portfolio as a whole, obviously, but that's optimized to the risk level and probably the other offers that the customer is getting.

187
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:55,440
And then maybe give us just to be, you know, evals are always a very hot topic, you kind of led with that.

188
00:15:55,900 --> 00:16:06,360
What is an example of maybe a hard-to-design area for evals or one where you had to spend an extra amount of time with just given the fact that, like, there's real money PII at risk?

189
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:06,500
Right.

190
00:16:06,760 --> 00:16:06,920
Yeah.

191
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:23,030
So when we decided to redesign the company around AI, you asked the question, okay, is AI going to be able to do this job, like even the CEO job or jobs where the leadership is?

192
00:16:23,410 --> 00:16:25,710
And the answer, honestly, is probably yes.

193
00:16:25,830 --> 00:16:28,850
Like in 2035 with a rate of improvement, it will be able to do.

194
00:16:28,850 --> 00:16:30,930
So we said, okay, let's try it now.

195
00:16:31,750 --> 00:16:34,410
Let's try and build an AI CEO.

196
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:38,560
So we carved out a city in Mexico.

197
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:39,820
It's Cuernavaca.

198
00:16:39,960 --> 00:16:44,970
And we put like an agent in one of our harnesses as a CEO.

199
00:16:45,170 --> 00:16:49,990
And it starts learning and it starts making decisions and evaluating on those decisions.

200
00:16:50,570 --> 00:16:53,430
And it's only been running for six weeks now.

201
00:16:53,430 --> 00:16:58,550
The goal of the first month was to double the profits of Cuernavaca.

202
00:16:58,890 --> 00:17:09,850
It didn't reach it, but it was 1.5x, like 50% more profits just by managing the city, which is crazy, right?

203
00:17:09,910 --> 00:17:10,650
It's amazing.

204
00:17:10,910 --> 00:17:12,090
And it's the CEO.

205
00:17:12,430 --> 00:17:16,810
Like people were like, that was the last job AI was supposed to take.

206
00:17:16,910 --> 00:17:18,270
And no, it isn't really.

207
00:17:18,810 --> 00:17:20,590
And how did this happen?

208
00:17:20,590 --> 00:17:40,860
And it's like a very smart person, like Fields Medal-level smart, like going into every single number, every single customer, making the perfect forecast and going to micromanage every single thing that needs to be executed every day to reach a plan.

209
00:17:40,860 --> 00:17:52,680
So he'll literally send messages to all the physical workers in Cuernavaca with their plans for the day and ask them to send voice notes back to know their progress.

210
00:17:53,520 --> 00:17:55,660
So customer satisfaction grew.

211
00:17:56,420 --> 00:17:57,900
We got a better inventory.

212
00:17:58,260 --> 00:18:03,400
We rotated better, better financing penetration, like every KPI started to improve.

213
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:06,280
So it's super cool and super exciting.

214
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:15,440
Now, what are the jobs where we think we're still like training and hiring humans?

215
00:18:15,980 --> 00:18:17,840
Those are related to the physical world.

216
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:24,040
So when we talk about mechanics, Cuernavaca has around, I think in Mexico, around 800 mechanics.

217
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:29,860
There's lots of dexterity and senses that's super hard to substitute.

218
00:18:30,240 --> 00:18:33,960
So there, we also build these agents with the exact same harness that's scaling.

219
00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:37,720
And the mechanics have the sidekick.

220
00:18:37,720 --> 00:18:48,500
I was telling you guys earlier, it's like the movie Ratatouille, like the mouse that's actually a chef collaborating with a human.

221
00:18:48,900 --> 00:18:49,900
It's kind of like that.

222
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:50,760
So it's a sidekick.

223
00:18:50,940 --> 00:18:51,960
We call it El Mike.

224
00:18:52,420 --> 00:18:59,880
And it tells them how to inspect a car and gives them tips and shows them the way to do it.

225
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:07,500
And the quality of inspections, again, went through the roof, we're inspecting faster, we're repairing faster, it's cheaper.

226
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:11,360
But most importantly, we're delivering higher quality cars.

227
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:19,460
Warranties came down around like 20, 26% since we launched.

228
00:19:19,700 --> 00:19:22,820
And customer satisfaction, again, went up.

229
00:19:23,100 --> 00:19:24,880
So it's about this.

230
00:19:24,880 --> 00:19:36,420
Like, how would you design your organization from scratch with abundant superintelligence that's cheap and just go build it?

231
00:19:37,260 --> 00:19:51,180
Now, this is a good segue to a key topic right now in Silicon Valley where, you know, there's a lot of people worried about how the organizations of the future are going to look like and the role that humans are going to play.

232
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:51,460
Yes.

233
00:19:51,740 --> 00:19:53,540
And I think you touched a little bit on that.

234
00:19:53,540 --> 00:19:57,120
And so we'd love to hear, yeah, like how you guys are thinking about that.

235
00:19:57,340 --> 00:19:57,600
Yes.

236
00:19:58,280 --> 00:20:00,520
And the organizations.

237
00:20:00,840 --> 00:20:00,940
Yeah.

238
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:01,720
Totally.

239
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:06,460
So we took that question very seriously three years ago.

240
00:20:06,720 --> 00:20:11,120
And the truth is that everyone's job will change.

241
00:20:12,300 --> 00:20:21,690
So, and what we were doing a couple of years ago will probably be performed better by an AI agent.

242
00:20:22,090 --> 00:20:22,350
Right?

243
00:20:22,410 --> 00:20:23,290
So what does this mean?

244
00:20:23,290 --> 00:20:25,900
Like, we need to train everyone.

245
00:20:26,640 --> 00:20:36,220
So, so we launched a program inside Kavak that's called the Jedi Academy where anyone from Kavak, like from the CEO to, yeah.

246
00:20:36,220 --> 00:20:36,980
And it's awesome.

247
00:20:37,220 --> 00:20:43,500
Like from the CEO to like AI engineers to mechanics, like going to the academy.

248
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:49,180
It's super hard. Like, I've led it myself.

249
00:20:49,340 --> 00:20:50,400
You designed the program.

250
00:20:50,460 --> 00:20:51,400
I designed the program.

251
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:52,540
But constantly.

252
00:20:52,620 --> 00:20:53,000
Constantly.

253
00:20:53,180 --> 00:20:57,120
Because you need to be upgrading the program because everything's changing so fast.

254
00:20:57,120 --> 00:21:04,620
And there's like, you can't send these people like outside to Stanford to, to learn this because like, it's new stuff.

255
00:21:04,620 --> 00:21:04,900
Right?

256
00:21:05,300 --> 00:21:07,120
So we train everyone.

257
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:14,920
And after six weeks, they launch state-of-the-art agents, AI agents to production.

258
00:21:14,920 --> 00:21:21,700
And it's mechanics and finance guys and engineers, like everyone can do it.

259
00:21:21,700 --> 00:21:27,520
And what this generated is maybe this person won't become an AI engineer.

260
00:21:27,680 --> 00:21:32,040
Some of them have, but they, they know how to collaborate with this new technology.

261
00:21:32,300 --> 00:21:32,460
Right?

262
00:21:32,560 --> 00:21:36,580
So the way we looked about it was, guys, there, there's no way back.

263
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:38,220
Like this is the way Kavak is going.

264
00:21:38,540 --> 00:21:40,080
This is the way the company will look like.

265
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:46,700
These are the changes for the engineering team, the finance team, the product team.

266
00:21:46,700 --> 00:21:48,340
Like, this is what's going to change.

267
00:21:49,690 --> 00:21:56,890
You have the choice to like train and, and get the skills to perform in this new reality, in this new world.

268
00:21:58,030 --> 00:22:01,750
Or maybe leave Kavak if this is not for you, but this is the way we're going.

269
00:22:02,350 --> 00:22:03,990
And we're great.

270
00:22:04,210 --> 00:22:07,710
Like, like we, we, we strengthened the culture and we were super excited.

271
00:22:08,730 --> 00:22:12,790
And people really know how to build these agentic systems.

272
00:22:12,790 --> 00:22:19,270
And then if you look at Kavak now, any process, it's really a collaboration of agents and humans.

273
00:22:19,390 --> 00:22:23,150
And sometimes like agents are the bosses or, of, of humans.

274
00:22:23,150 --> 00:22:25,230
And sometimes humans are designing the agents.

275
00:22:25,890 --> 00:22:29,880
But I think we, we managed to really build this and change this.

276
00:22:30,440 --> 00:22:38,960
And, and it's through this idea that we need to be learning every day and things will continue to change.

277
00:22:38,960 --> 00:22:46,620
And the only way to, to continue being relevant is to upgrade your skills every month or every couple of months.

278
00:22:46,940 --> 00:22:50,840
But you do have, or did have, you know, thousands of people.

279
00:22:51,180 --> 00:22:53,320
Now agents do most things.

280
00:22:53,320 --> 00:22:53,620
Yes.

281
00:22:53,860 --> 00:22:56,860
So like, what is the org structure of Kavak?

282
00:22:57,100 --> 00:22:59,620
Like, does the middle management concept even exist anymore?

283
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:00,640
Like, what does your org look like?

284
00:23:00,740 --> 00:23:00,960
Right.

285
00:23:01,100 --> 00:23:06,210
So the way it looks like now is very flat teams, very senior teams, super empowered.

286
00:23:06,210 --> 00:23:13,490
If you look at a team, you'll have engineering, AI, like operations, like everything.

287
00:23:13,770 --> 00:23:21,990
And they're either building the agents, working for the agents, or being in the physical world in front of the customer.

288
00:23:22,650 --> 00:23:24,810
Like most of our organization looks like that.

289
00:23:24,950 --> 00:23:36,070
So, so it's really built around, around the idea of how organizations will look like in the future and around AI and really harnessing this, this new technology.

290
00:23:36,690 --> 00:23:43,010
Obviously, this required lots of retraining because in 2023 or 2022, no one was building agents.

291
00:23:43,430 --> 00:23:47,170
No one was helping agents or taking orders from, from agents.

292
00:23:47,350 --> 00:23:56,810
And the way you cater to the physical world or the customers was in a different way than if an agent's telling you what to do or helping you make your job better.

293
00:23:56,810 --> 00:23:57,090
Yep.

294
00:23:57,090 --> 00:24:01,310
And so it's a completely different structure than, than we had just two years ago.

295
00:24:01,390 --> 00:24:01,510
Yeah.

296
00:24:01,890 --> 00:24:05,710
Explain, we talked about this before, what working for the agents look like.

297
00:24:05,790 --> 00:24:07,910
Like, I think the way you described it was an agentic system.

298
00:24:08,050 --> 00:24:11,470
And then sometimes when it fails, it's like, oh, that's kicked out to kind of a human queue.

299
00:24:11,550 --> 00:24:11,730
Right.

300
00:24:11,730 --> 00:24:12,950
But then that's lost.

301
00:24:13,050 --> 00:24:14,110
And so how have you brought that together?

302
00:24:14,350 --> 00:24:22,460
So like the, the, we see human in the loops and, and, and most of these agentic systems in production right now, like large scale agentic systems.

303
00:24:22,460 --> 00:24:33,920
Usually if an agent hits a wall or can't perform anymore, it'll like send the, this case or this customer to a tier two support and forget about it.

304
00:24:34,080 --> 00:24:37,640
That doesn't really work because you don't close the loops.

305
00:24:37,640 --> 00:24:41,240
So you don't generate the data to train the agent to do this better.

306
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:46,540
What works right now is we have an agent that's obsessed with each of the customers, like millions of this.

307
00:24:46,540 --> 00:24:55,000
They have access to every single API, every single skill, like, and we have agents building those humans, building those skills for them.

308
00:24:55,560 --> 00:25:02,060
And then if an agent hits a wall or cancel something, it'll call this API saying, I need help.

309
00:25:02,340 --> 00:25:05,900
And on the other side, it's not an agent or software.

310
00:25:06,020 --> 00:25:08,180
It's a human helping them out.

311
00:25:08,340 --> 00:25:14,020
But if you map this out in an org chart, it's really human teams that have an agent.

312
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:15,300
I'm getting better results.

313
00:25:15,300 --> 00:25:16,720
It's super clear.

314
00:25:16,840 --> 00:25:18,260
Like, it makes sense.

315
00:25:18,260 --> 00:25:19,340
That's actually a perfect segue.

316
00:25:19,460 --> 00:25:24,120
And I know you get lots of leaders at larger institutions inbounding to you.

317
00:25:24,300 --> 00:25:25,840
So maybe this will save you many phone calls.

318
00:25:25,900 --> 00:25:31,880
But I think rationally, many leaders of companies intuitively understand this.

319
00:25:31,940 --> 00:25:37,060
It is very hard still to deploy AI through their organization.

320
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:38,380
Like, the models are good enough.

321
00:25:38,460 --> 00:25:39,000
You know that.

322
00:25:39,140 --> 00:25:40,160
It's an org problem.

323
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:41,300
It's a psychology problem.

324
00:25:41,400 --> 00:25:43,700
Like, what advice do you have or what have you seen?

325
00:25:43,700 --> 00:25:45,200
I think it's two things.

326
00:25:45,540 --> 00:25:49,880
The first is it has to be top down because of this.

327
00:25:50,080 --> 00:26:04,260
Like, if you just get adoption, it won't go anywhere because it's hard to generate this taste or strategy for people to bottom up, decide what to build and whatnot, and come up with something that works for the company.

328
00:26:04,260 --> 00:26:12,180
So the transformation has to be top down, and leaders need to adopt, and leaders have to have a very clear plan on what to build.

329
00:26:12,660 --> 00:26:14,380
I've seen so many companies.

330
00:26:14,620 --> 00:26:16,760
It's just like, oh, like, we're doing a hackathon.

331
00:26:16,960 --> 00:26:18,360
People are coming up with use cases.

332
00:26:18,360 --> 00:26:20,500
We're sponsoring some of these use cases.

333
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:21,660
That doesn't work.

334
00:26:21,840 --> 00:26:30,570
It's like, be very clear on what the company will look like in three or five years, and then start building that.

335
00:26:30,710 --> 00:26:34,910
And be, like, very vertical in guiding your troops towards that.

336
00:26:35,030 --> 00:26:42,670
Like, an army doesn't really work if everyone comes up with ideas on the strategy and tactics and goes to the battlefield and, like, does whatever they want.

337
00:26:42,670 --> 00:26:45,950
Like, you need a very clear strategy, and that's what we need now.

338
00:26:46,010 --> 00:26:47,670
It's at, like, transformation stage.

339
00:26:48,190 --> 00:26:53,830
The second one is you need to measure what really matters, and it's evals, but it's also the right evals.

340
00:26:54,270 --> 00:27:00,450
So I see a lot of companies spending now huge amounts, and they say, okay, I got adoption.

341
00:27:00,790 --> 00:27:04,730
I'm just spending, like, hundreds of millions of dollars in tokens now.

342
00:27:04,990 --> 00:27:05,870
What about that?

343
00:27:06,030 --> 00:27:07,930
Like, there's quality in the tokens.

344
00:27:07,930 --> 00:27:10,570
So I have a framework here that's also useful.

345
00:27:11,230 --> 00:27:21,160
Like, tier three tokens, the most valuable, are these agents where you can get the ROI of each specific token.

346
00:27:21,300 --> 00:27:22,220
And I can do that now.

347
00:27:22,300 --> 00:27:23,480
That's great news for me.

348
00:27:24,020 --> 00:27:32,460
Because I'm growing, and because I know the ROI of each token, because it goes to agents that are performing the job of the organization, right?

349
00:27:32,520 --> 00:27:33,500
These are the best tokens.

350
00:27:33,920 --> 00:27:38,320
Tier two tokens are things that you can measure indirectly.

351
00:27:38,520 --> 00:27:40,880
Do I see devs in the code base?

352
00:27:41,180 --> 00:27:45,780
And I can evaluate the value of these tokens, at least indirectly, and then push those to productions.

353
00:27:46,560 --> 00:27:53,180
Tier one, when most companies are, is people are just using plug code or chat GPT or co-work or whatever.

354
00:27:53,580 --> 00:27:54,360
What happened with those?

355
00:27:54,560 --> 00:27:55,700
I have no idea.

356
00:27:56,320 --> 00:27:57,980
So it's not just about adoption.

357
00:27:57,980 --> 00:28:08,580
It's really about having a very clear vision and then measuring that each token you spend is bringing you those benefits and just iterate, iterate, iterate from there.

358
00:28:08,580 --> 00:28:22,760
And we touched on this a little bit, but I think it's worth a dive as maybe the most ambitious companies listening to this will decide to follow suit, which is you decided to build an agent per customer versus per task.

359
00:28:22,760 --> 00:28:29,100
And then discovered along the way that each one of those agents needs its own micro-virtual machine.

360
00:28:29,320 --> 00:28:33,700
So maybe kind of walk us through those decisions, that architecture.

361
00:28:33,700 --> 00:28:51,680
And I think we're seeing these results now, but it was a really risky bet because people usually go from workflows, like if I could advise everyone, don't build agentic workflows, to graphs or functions or objectives.

362
00:28:51,680 --> 00:28:57,960
And we built that, that these are multi-agent systems that can perform a whole function for a complex goals.

363
00:28:58,180 --> 00:29:04,760
Like the ones I told you that to sell a car, you need to do financing, purchasing, like recommendations, et cetera.

364
00:29:05,380 --> 00:29:11,120
And we had thousands, like tens of thousands of these agents working at scale, running the business back in December.

365
00:29:11,120 --> 00:29:18,360
But then Opus 4.5 came out and I realized like, this isn't the right paradigm anymore.

366
00:29:19,160 --> 00:29:30,880
Like the intelligence now doesn't need like the graph and the multi-agent lattice work and harness because it will constrain this level of intelligence.

367
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:54,200
So we decided to like destroy everything we had been building for two years that was working, that brought us to profitability, that brought us amazing growth and start over with a harness that we thought would be robust and scalable and leverage recursive self-improvement or new models, more intelligent models coming out every month.

368
00:29:54,200 --> 00:30:08,840
So the way this looks like it's a virtual machine with an agent, with access to memory and evals and the CLI where they can access every tool and every API in my company and the long-term goal.

369
00:30:09,220 --> 00:30:17,110
And I instantiate hundreds of thousands of these each day with long-term goals, like maximizing the lifetime value.

370
00:30:17,270 --> 00:30:19,050
The self-improving organization.

371
00:30:19,110 --> 00:30:19,390
Exactly.

372
00:30:19,550 --> 00:30:20,550
The self-improving organization.

373
00:30:20,550 --> 00:30:29,430
And I think people are super obsessed with RSI now, and this will improve the models.

374
00:30:30,010 --> 00:30:41,840
But if you look at it this way, economic value in humanity for the past 4,000 years has been delivered by organization, not by individuals.

375
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:49,260
So what you want to self-improve and to engage in that loop is the organization that can deliver more economic value, right?

376
00:30:49,260 --> 00:31:13,560
So that's the loop that I think companies will start to focus on because if you get that loop working and it's an organization that is really self-improving and harnessing the newer models and the better intelligence that we're getting every couple of days now, then you hit the exponential, not just in intelligence, but in the value that you can generate as a company.

377
00:31:13,560 --> 00:31:15,560
So that's really exciting.

378
00:31:15,560 --> 00:31:28,060
You mentioned that because of all the challenges in adopting AI, you saw the biggest opportunity on net new companies being formed working on this new way and then disrupting markets.

379
00:31:28,300 --> 00:31:29,660
You want to talk a little bit about that?

380
00:31:29,760 --> 00:31:30,020
Yes.

381
00:31:30,020 --> 00:31:37,470
There's this concept in economics about creative destruction from Joseph Schumpeter.

382
00:31:38,190 --> 00:31:56,090
And what it says is that the way innovation hits the economy isn't by companies adopting the new technology, but by companies remaining the way they were and incumbents with the new technology destroying the old companies.

383
00:31:56,090 --> 00:32:08,510
So this destroys value in the short term in the economy, but in the long term, it's better for everyone because these new, more efficient, more effective companies will provide better products and services for the economy as a whole.

384
00:32:08,950 --> 00:32:13,110
And this has happened in the past industrial revolutions, and this has always happened.

385
00:32:13,110 --> 00:32:23,880
And it's a great opportunity for entrepreneurs and people today because it's hard to adopt AI deeply.

386
00:32:24,950 --> 00:32:32,950
It's really hard for a CEO today, especially of a large company or public company, to go and say, hey, like I'm betting everything on AI.

387
00:32:32,950 --> 00:32:44,650
The company has to look this way, I'll destroy and rebuild everything I've been building for the past 40 years to become an AI native company.

388
00:32:45,090 --> 00:32:48,410
Like how many CEOs will do that in a company at scale?

389
00:32:48,410 --> 00:33:03,400
So while they adopt, new companies can be formed that are built around the strengths of AI and take over and bring new products and services to the masses.

390
00:33:04,160 --> 00:33:06,000
And this has happened before.

391
00:33:06,260 --> 00:33:07,520
Like this happened with electricity.

392
00:33:07,520 --> 00:33:09,620
This is a story I always tell my team.

393
00:33:10,620 --> 00:33:18,080
The technologies for Ford's production line were developed in 1879 and 1881.

394
00:33:19,080 --> 00:33:27,500
Edison started commercializing electricity in New York and then London, and he invented a dynamo that was extremely efficient.

395
00:33:27,960 --> 00:33:34,970
So you could have built Ford's factory 40 years before Ford.

396
00:33:35,090 --> 00:33:35,870
The technology was there.

397
00:33:35,970 --> 00:33:36,590
Everything was there.

398
00:33:36,590 --> 00:33:49,090
But the way people adopted electricity and Ford's dynamo was, okay, I'm going to leave my factory like four floors, shafts and belts, and just change my coal engine for an electric engine.

399
00:33:49,090 --> 00:33:52,930
And this will bring you benefits, yes, but like 6% efficiency.

400
00:33:53,860 --> 00:34:10,060
What needed to be done was to destroy that factory, build it in a flat surface, not in the center of New York, but in Connecticut or New Jersey, and redesign your whole factory around small dynamos and electricity.

401
00:34:10,060 --> 00:34:20,040
And then you get like the 3x improvement in productivity that powered the U.S. during the 20th century.

402
00:34:20,280 --> 00:34:23,540
And the same happened again with the computer, and the same is happening again today.

403
00:34:23,620 --> 00:34:29,940
People want to adopt it, but they're not willing to redesign the whole company, and they just adopt it superficially.

404
00:34:29,940 --> 00:34:37,220
And in the end, that'll give you a 6% or a 10% improvement, not a 10x improvement.

405
00:34:37,420 --> 00:34:43,780
And it's like the innovator's dilemma at an industrial scale again.

406
00:34:45,240 --> 00:34:50,580
I think you've just made an amazing case for any future founders out there that it's time to build.

407
00:34:50,700 --> 00:34:51,360
It's time to build.

408
00:34:51,360 --> 00:34:56,200
And maybe a great place to end is, you know, you've built and scaled your own company.

409
00:34:56,400 --> 00:34:58,900
You've now turned Kavak fully agentic.

410
00:34:59,180 --> 00:35:03,680
Like, what advice do you have to future founders or first-time founders that might be listening?

411
00:35:04,140 --> 00:35:07,680
So this is the most exciting time in human history.

412
00:35:07,840 --> 00:35:08,620
I believe that.

413
00:35:08,760 --> 00:35:11,800
Like, we're living in the most exciting time in human history.

414
00:35:11,800 --> 00:35:29,680
And it's the most exciting time to be a founder because it's the first time that anyone has access to the most powerful tools and intelligence in the world, like, almost for free or for $20 a month.

415
00:35:29,920 --> 00:35:38,300
So literally, the democratization of the tools for people to build has never been this way in human history.

416
00:35:38,300 --> 00:35:45,980
And there's so much problems to be solved and a new reality to be built around this new paradigm.

417
00:35:46,260 --> 00:35:49,880
So say, like, just go for it, but go for it deep.

418
00:35:50,040 --> 00:35:53,660
Like, imagine what the future around the AI will look like.

419
00:35:54,790 --> 00:35:57,050
It's not even an exponential.

420
00:35:57,330 --> 00:36:04,010
Just map a trend that's linear if things keep getting, like, AI keeps getting better at a linear scale.

421
00:36:04,010 --> 00:36:11,890
And just build for that, and you'll come up with wonderful ideas that will, like, bring a lot of value to the world.

422
00:36:13,310 --> 00:36:13,710
